    "evidence_text": "Induction of myelodysplasia by myeloid-derived suppressor cells.\n\nBone progenitor dysfunction induces myelodysplasia and secondary leukemia\n\nMyelodysplasia resulted and acute myelogenous leukaemia emerged that had acquired several genetic abnormalities while having intact Dicer1.\n\nDeletion of Sbds in mouse osteoprogenitors induced bone marrow dysfunction with myelodysplasia.\n\nFurthermore, primary stromal dysfunction can result in secondary neoplastic disease, supporting the concept of niche-induced oncogenesis.\n\nLandscape of genetic lesions in 944 patients with myelodysplastic syndromes\n\nWe determined the biological and prognostic significance of genetic aberrations in MDS.\n\nMany mutations were associated with higher risk groups and/or blast elevation.\n\nSurvival was investigated in 875 patients.\n\nThus, large-scale genetic and molecular profiling of multiple target genes is invaluable for subclassification and prognostication in MDS patients.\n\nMyelodysplastic syndromes: revisiting the role of the bone marrow microenvironment in disease pathogenesis\n\nMyelodysplastic syndromes are a heterogeneous group of diseases characterized by ineffective hematopoiesis and the propensity to leukemic transformation.\n\nTheir pathogenesis is complex and likely depends on interplay between aberrant hematopoietic cells and their microenvironment.\n\nReactive oxygen species, DNA damage, and error-prone repair: a model for genomic instability with progression in myeloid leukemia?\n\nThe molecular basis for MDS progression is unknown, but a key element in MDS disease progression is loss of chromosomal material (genomic instability).\n\nThere is a concomitant increase in reactive oxygen species (ROS) in these transgenic mice with disease progression.\n\nThese data suggest treatment strategies that target RAS/RAC pathways and ROS production in human MDS/AML.\n\nThe NLRP3 inflammasome functions as a driver of the myelodysplastic syndrome phenotype.\n\nPerturbed ribosome biogenesis in DBA has been shown to induce a p53-mediated ribosomal stress response.\n\nHowever, the mechanisms of p53 activation and its relevance for the erythroid defect remain elusive.\n\nUnexpectedly, disruption of the 5S RNP\u2013Mdm2 interaction also led to selective defect in erythropoiesis.",
